    | General Notes | The preliminary results of a recent analysis of the Lao civil registration and vital statistics (CRVS) system by UNFPA found that the quality of the 2015 census’ enumerated deaths is questionable, and an under-reporting of births and deaths in the Lao CRVS.
    | Data Limitations | The population projections within this COD-PS contain underyling uncertainties, because it is unclear whether the National Statistical Office (NSO) actually conducted post-enumeration survey (PES) after the 2015 census to adjust the census results accordingly. Similarly, the NSO did not conduct a PES after the 2005 census. Therefore, the NSO's method of adjusting the 2015 census
    | COD-PS Demographic Data Quality Assessment | The COD-PS was assessed against ADM-0 estimates and projections published by the World Population Prospects (2022 Revision) from the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs (UN-DESA). Projected fertility, child mortality, and interprovincial migration trends were evaluated against the Lao Social Indicator Survey 2017 (MICS/DHS), and fou
    sheet 'Methodological Documentation': 9 rows x 2 cols
    | Item | Methodological_Documentation
    | Methodology Used | Cohort component method was applied to the results of the 2015 census to build projections on which this COD-PS is based.
    | Baseline Population | 2015 census
    | Post-enumeration survey (PES) | Although the 2015 census report notes that a post-enumeration survey (PES) was conducted immediately after the 2015 census field operations and that the PES report would be published separately, such a PES report cannot be located in the public sphere.
    | Assessment and Adjustment of the Baseline Population | Prior to projecting forward, the 2015 census population was found to have a wide range of age misreporting and under-enumeration of children 0-4 and 5-9 years old. The under-enumeration of children 0-9 years old in the 2015 census was adjusted by retrospectively estimating the annual numbers of women of reproductive age dating back to the 2005
    | Fertility (births) | Baseline national-level fertility was derived from the mid-year total fertility rate (TFR) and age-specific fertility rates (ASFR) published in the 2015 census. Baseline subnational mid-year TFRs and ASFRs were first derived from the unadjusted 2015 census data and adjusted by the ratio of the adjusted national ASFR to unadjusted national ASFR, then further refined to be consi
    | Mortality (deaths) | The baseline national mortality level was derived from the estimates of life expectancy published in the census report (63.0 years for males and 66.4 years for females in 2015). Subnational mortality assumptions were derived by determining a life table for each province from the Coale-Demeny West model life tables, with the age-sex population distribution and adjusted crude de
    | International migration (net migration) | At the time of buidling the projections, international migration was assumed to have a negligable impact on the national total population during the entire projection period and was not factored into these projections. This was due to lack of reliable data on international migraton and evidence suggesting that the number of emigrants recorded in the census
    | Internal migration (migration within country) | Interprovincial migration was measured using 2005-2015 census data. For subnational units, the level of net migration and migrant age structure for each province was determined using the 2015 census. Most internal migration occurred among persons aged 15-35, and migration was somewhat more common for males than females. The proportion of interprovinc
